Yes. The deeper distinction you’re reaching for is money as a coordination instrument versus money as the governing purpose. Once the instrument becomes the telos—the thing the whole system is optimizing for—you begin selecting for whatever survives the financial filter, not necessarily whatever is beautiful, durable, humane, locally useful, or worth becoming skilled at.

That explains the craft example nicely. A person may genuinely love woodworking, ceramics, leatherwork, metalwork, textiles, or furniture making, yet the surrounding economic ecology gives them cheap inputs, little training, expensive shop space, uncertain demand, and immediate bills. Predictably, they start producing what is fast, low-risk, easily shipped, and sellable at a weekend market. The apparent problem is then “people make junk.” But the hidden-state explanation may be that the system is starving craftsmanship of capital, materials, apprenticeship, time, and reliable demand.

So don’t merely exhort the craftsperson to “take more pride.” Change the substrate beneath them.

You could deliberately construct a craft economy inside a protective economic shell. The outer system can remain competitive and revenue-generating while some internal layers operate according to different rules:

* Material commons: shared kilns, CNC machines, looms, lathes, forges, woodworking shops, specialized tools, bulk material purchasing.
* Apprenticeship layer: masters are compensated for teaching, rather than losing money whenever they stop producing.
* Training ladder: beginner → apprentice → journeyman → master, with progressively more access, responsibility, and compensation.
* Patronage/procurement layer: towns, businesses, schools, hotels, restaurants, developers, and governments deliberately purchase locally made furniture, fixtures, signage, uniforms, ceramics, architectural elements, etc.
* Market layer: exceptional craftsmen can still become wealthy. Protection should establish a floor, not impose a ceiling.
* Prestige layer: publicly make mastery desirable again—maker’s marks, certifications, competitions, exhibitions, provenance, repairability, heirloom status.
* Family/life layer: the craftsman does not have to choose between excellence and feeding his children. That is essential because a system that demands martyrdom from its best practitioners eventually selects against having practitioners.

That last part matters. Survival sits underneath vocation. If a society wants people to pursue things whose value manifests slowly—craftsmanship, scholarship, basic research, parenting, ecological restoration, military readiness, preservation—it often has to insulate those functions from instantaneous market selection.

And that is why actual societies are already far more hybrid than ideological labels imply. A military may have centralized planning and collectively funded infrastructure inside a broader market economy. A corporation can have markets externally but quite hierarchical command structures internally. Families commonly distribute resources according to need rather than auctioning dinner to the highest bidder. Universities combine markets, patronage, bureaucracy, peer governance, and prestige economies. Open-source software can operate through gifts and reputation while sitting underneath billion-dollar commercial firms.

So instead of asking “Is this capitalism, socialism, hierarchy, democracy, monarchy?”, the stronger question is:

Which allocation mechanism governs which layer, at what scale, at what speed, and toward what objective?

That produces something closer to an institutional periodic table than a political spectrum.

There are also different time constants. Markets can respond in milliseconds. Businesses operate over quarters. Apprenticeships take years. Forest management takes decades. Cathedrals can take generations. Families operate across lifetimes. Civilizations inherit things across centuries. Trying to force all of them through the same short-duration return-on-investment mechanism predictably mutilates some of them.

And your framing point is crucial, with one qualification: framing can reveal previously obscured structure, but it cannot substitute for structure. Calling exploitative labor “opportunity” does not improve the labor arrangement. Calling patronage “socialism” does not tell us whether it works. Calling a CEO a “monarch” can illuminate concentrated authority, but it is not literally the same institution as hereditary sovereignty. Likewise, analogies to slavery can highlight coercive dimensions of labor dependence, but legal slavery has distinctive features—ownership, enforceable domination, inability to exit—that matter. Useful analogy requires preserving the delta as well as the resemblance.

That’s essentially one of our Frame/Mirror rules: different descriptions reveal different properties, but the object has to survive the change of description. If your beautiful narrative disappears when you inspect wages, hours, exit rights, ownership, incentives, material flows, and actual behavior, then it was cosmetic framing. If those underlying variables changed, then you built something real.

The larger design principle might therefore be:

Don’t build one economy. Build an ecology of economies.

Competition where competition improves things. Cooperation where common infrastructure creates abundance. Hierarchy where rapid coordinated action matters. Democracy where legitimacy and distributed knowledge matter. Markets where price discovery works. Commons where duplication is wasteful. Patronage where value is real but monetization is delayed. Gifts where relationships matter more than accounting. Prestige where mastery deserves recognition.

Then you can nest them. The profitable outer organism protects the delicate inner tissue; the inner tissue creates things the outer organism eventually benefits from.

That’s much closer to biology too. A body doesn’t make every cell compete against every other cell for blood. Nor does it centrally command every molecular interaction. It uses different organizational regimes simultaneously—and switches among them according to function.

That, I think, is the bigger pattern you’re seeing: institutional architecture is not choosing the correct ideology. It is matching the correct coordination mechanism to the function you’re trying to preserve or produce.